{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"center","type":"text","text":"# OSKG-YahWeh: What the Graph Shows\n\n723 claims · 17 scholars · 11 primary sources\n4-phase structural analysis → 8 settled convergences + 4 genuine fault lines\n\n**Method:** Hinge inventory → cascade trees (BFS) → counter-position stress tests → convergence detection\n**Threshold:** HIGH+ supports, zero MEDIUM+ contradicts = settled. Bidirectional HIGH contradicts = genuine fault line.\n\n[[notes/synthesis/capstone-what-does-this-mean]]","x":-740,"y":-2080,"width":500,"height":340,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"conv-bes-figures","type":"text","text":"## Bes Figures Are NOT Yahweh and Asherah\n\nKuntillet Ajrud pithoi: Bes ≠ divine couple.\n\n- Iconographic analysis: figures match known Bes-type dwarf-deity depictions\n- Bes: Egyptian apotropaic household god — NOT a high god with consort\n- No ANE parallel for Yahweh depicted anthropomorphically in this period\n- Two Bes figures (male + female-type) = standard Bes/Beset pairing\n- Earlier \"Yahweh and Asherah\" identification now rejected by iconography specialists\n\n**Keel & Uehlinger (HIGH) · Smith (HIGH) · Zevit**\n\n6 HIGH+ supports · 0 MEDIUM+ contradicts — iconographic consensus: Bes","x":-1540,"y":-2800,"width":340,"height":620,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"conv-parallel-religions","type":"text","text":"## Ancient Israel Had Parallel Religions\n\nElite (book) religion ≠ folk (practiced) religion.\n\n- Kuntillet Ajrud: \"Yahweh and his Asherah\" + Bes figures\n- 1000s of Judean Pillar Figurines (8th-7th c. BCE) in domestic contexts\n- Elephantine Papyri: 5th c. Jewish community worships Anat-Yahu\n- Jeremiah 44: elite condemnation of Queen of Heaven worship\n- Standing stones (massebot) at domestic sites\n\n**Dever (HIGH, 5+) · Smith (HIGH) · Stavrakopoulou (HIGH) · Keel & Uehlinger (HIGH)**\n\n10 HIGH+ supports · 0 MEDIUM+ contradicts — standard paradigm","x":-2160,"y":-2000,"width":340,"height":640,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"conv-monotheism-transformed","type":"text","text":"## Monotheism Transformed Canaanite Myth\n\nDidn't reject it — absorbed and reworked it.\n\n- Ps 74:13-14: Yahweh crushing Leviathan = Baal crushing Litanu (Ugaritic)\n- Ps 29: almost certainly a Baal hymn reattributed to Yahweh\n- Exod 15 (Song of the Sea): mirrors Baal's victory over Sea/Yamm\n- Ps 82: divine council inherited from Canaanite pantheon structure\n- Chaoskampf: creation-through-combat pattern shared across ANE, rewritten for Yahweh\n\n**Smith (HIGH, 4+) · Day (HIGH, 3+) · Cross (HIGH) · Romer**\n\n8 HIGH+ supports · 0 MEDIUM+ contradicts — \"Canaanite inheritance\" well-established","x":-2080,"y":-780,"width":340,"height":620,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"conv-jerusalem-centralization","type":"text","text":"## Jerusalem Centralization Was Late Political Project\n\nJosiah's reform (late 7th c. BCE) — not Mosaic.\n\n- 2 Kings 22-23: \"Book of the Law\" discovered during temple renovation\n- All high places (bamot) shut down; sacrifice centralized to Jerusalem alone\n- Arad temple: deliberately decommissioned late 7th c. — archaeological confirmation\n- Regional shrines active through 8th c., abandoned late 7th\n- Deut 12: single sanctuary command — theological warrant for centralization\n\n**Romer (HIGH) · Albertz (HIGH) · Smith · Dever**\n\n7 HIGH+ supports · 0 MEDIUM+ contradicts — Josianic reform as political project settled","x":230,"y":-3600,"width":340,"height":640,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"conv-yahweh-southern","type":"text","text":"## Yahweh Originated in Southern Deserts\n\nEdom / Midian / Seir / Teman.\n\n- Deut 33:2: \"Yahweh came from Sinai, dawned from Seir\"\n- Judg 5:4-5: Yahweh marches from Seir/Edom\n- Hab 3:3: \"God comes from Teman\"\n- Kuntillet Ajrud: \"Yahweh of Teman\" inscription\n- Soleb Shasu: \"Shasu of Yhw\" (14th c. BCE) in Edom/Seir region\n- Midianite-Kenite hypothesis (early 20th c.)\n\n**Smith (HIGH) · Romer (HIGH) · Day (HIGH) · Dever · Cross · Fleming**\n\n6+ scholars HIGH+ · 0 MEDIUM+ contradicts — scholarly default position","x":570,"y":-2800,"width":340,"height":560,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"conv-archaeology-primary","type":"text","text":"## Archaeology Is Primary Source for Folk Religion\n\nTexts record elite theology; ground truth is material.\n\n- ~1000 JPFs: what were they for? Not in biblical texts\n- Cult stands from Taanach (10th c.): imagery absent from textual record\n- Domestic shrines at Tell Beit Mirsim, Tell Halif, Tell en-Nasbeh\n- Mortuary evidence: bench tombs with grave goods, feeding-the-dead\n- Biblical texts polemicize AGAINST these practices — confirming they existed\n\n**Dever (HIGH) · Keel & Uehlinger (HIGH) · Zevit (HIGH) · Stavrakopoulou (HIGH)**\n\n9 HIGH+ supports · 0 MEDIUM+ contradicts — methodological consensus","x":570,"y":-2180,"width":340,"height":580,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"fault-deut32","type":"text","text":"## Deut 32:8-9: One God or Two?\n\n**Hinge #3 · 65 dependents · collapse radius 4 levels**\n\n### Position A: ONE GOD (Heiser, MEDIUM, 1 support)\nElyon IS Yahweh — \"Most High\" is epithet, not separate deity. MT \"sons of Israel\" is original. LXX \"sons of God\" is interpretive corruption.\n\n### Position B: TWO GODS (Smith, HIGH, 2 supports)\nDSS/LXX: \"sons of God\" — Elyon apportions nations; Yahweh receives Israel. Two distinct deities in original text. MT \"sons of Israel\" is theological correction. Matches Ugaritic pattern: El (Elyon) presiding, assigning domains to lesser gods.\n\n### Evidence Types in Conflict\nText-critical (DSS/LXX/MT) · Comparative ANE (Ugaritic divine council) · Theological (MT redactional tendency)","x":2680,"y":1440,"width":360,"height":760,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"conv-el-family","type":"text","text":"## El Was Family/Clan Deity vs. National Yahweh\n\nFundamentally different divine profiles.\n\n- El: \"Kindly, Wise\" — elderly patriarch, family-oriented, fatherly\n- Yahweh: storm-warrior from the south, young, aggressive, national\n- Ugaritic texts: El as elder council president; Baal as young warrior\n  — Israel inherits BOTH profiles, later merges them\n- Gen 49:25: El's blessings — \"breasts and womb\" (fertility/family)\n- Exod 15:3: \"Yahweh is a man of war\" (martial/national)\n\n**Smith (HIGH, 4+) · Cross (HIGH) · Day (HIGH) · Romer**\n\n6 HIGH+ supports · 0 MEDIUM+ contradicts — profile differentiation textually robust","x":-580,"y":320,"width":340,"height":620,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"fault-israelite-uniqueness","type":"text","text":"## Israelite Uniqueness: Radically Different or ANE-Normal?\n\n### Position A: RADICALLY DIFFERENT (Kaufmann, MEDIUM, 9 supports)\nIsraelite religion non-mythological at core. Pagan categories don't apply. Monotheism as sui generis breakthrough.\n\n### Position B: ANE-NORMAL (Smith, MEDIUM-HIGH, 4 supports)\nIsrael shared ANE cultural repertoire. Divine council, Chaoskampf, temple ideology, covenant forms — all have ANE parallels. Uniqueness emerged gradually; difference is in degree and trajectory, not kind.\n\n### Methodological Split\nKaufmann: theological/textual primacy, internal categories\nSmith: comparative historical method, external ANE context\n\n### Connected to ALL other fault lines\nAsherah (ANE goddess parallels) · Deut 32:8-9 (Ugaritic divine council) · Monotheism timing (was early Israel fundamentally different?)","x":4720,"y":140,"width":340,"height":980,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"fault-monotheism-timing","type":"text","text":"## Monotheism: Early Revolution or Late Evolution?\n\n### Position A: EARLY REVOLUTION (Kaufmann)\nMonotheism was Israel's foundational idea, radically new from start. Israelite religion fundamentally non-mythological. Pagan parallels are superficial.\n\n**0 support edges from other scholars · 37+ contradict edges (HIGH)**\n\n### Position B: LATE EVOLUTION (Romer, Smith)\nMonotheism emerged gradually: monolatry → henotheism → true monotheism. Not achieved until Deutero-Isaiah (6th c. BCE): \"I am Yahweh, there is no other\" (Isa 45:5). Earlier texts freely acknowledge other gods (Exod 15:11).\n\n**4 supports (HIGH) · critical consensus**\n\n### Hinge #2: 42 downstream dependents","x":3760,"y":-1910,"width":340,"height":730,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"fault-asherah","type":"text","text":"## Asherah: Goddess or Symbol?\n\n**LARGEST FAULT LINE — bidirectional HIGH contradicts**\n\n### Position A: SYMBOL (Smith, 14 HIGH supports)\n\"His asherah\" = cult object, not goddess. Possessive suffix on proper name unattested in ANE. No Iron IIB goddess iconography in Judah. Asherah as wooden pole/stylized tree (Deut 16:21).\n\n### Position B: GODDESS (Dever 5+, Romer 4+, Day 3+)\nBlessing formula pairs Asherah with Yahweh like divine couple. Khirbet el-Qom parallel confirms same formula. Suffix not grammatically impossible — can name deity's cult symbol. Pre-exilic popular religion knew a goddess.\n\n### Sub-Disputes\n- Kuntillet Ajrud grammar: suffix debate unresolved; both sides use same inscriptions\n- Iron IIB iconographic gap: goddess proponents cite preservation bias + aniconic tendency\n\n**Structural:** Different epistemological standards between sides (textual vs. iconographic vs. archaeological primacy)","x":2680,"y":-1255,"width":360,"height":950,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"conv-el-original","type":"text","text":"## El Was Israel's Original God\n\nYahweh imported later — not indigenous to Israel.\n\n- Deut 32:8-9: Elyon apportions nations, Yahweh receives Israel\n- Name \"Israel\" = \"El strives\" (not Yahweh)\n- Patriarchal narratives use El epithets (El Shaddai, El Elyon, El Olam)\n- Exodus 6:3: \"I appeared as El Shaddai; by my name Yahweh I was not known\"\n- Ps 82: Yahweh stands in El's divine council\n\n**Smith (HIGH, 5+) · Day (HIGH, 4+) · Romer (HIGH, 3+) · Cross (HIGH)**\n\n16 HIGH+ supports · 0 MEDIUM+ contradicts — strongest single convergence","x":230,"y":-780,"width":340,"height":580,"color":"4"}
	],
